WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:03.650
AUDIENCE: One of my questions
is about GPU rendering.

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:05.650
And I wonder if any
of your solutions

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.770
are incorporating stuff
that OTOY is doing

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.300
or, you know, GPU
instances, is Google

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.530
planning on throwing in
graphics cards to machines,

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.110
and where are you
guys headed there.

00:00:17.197 --> 00:00:18.780
SRIKANTH BELWADI:
OK, this is actually

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.400
one of the most asked
features of rendering.

00:00:24.966 --> 00:00:27.340
What I can say is this is
something that we're definitely

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:27.890
looking at.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.760
Currently, most
of the rendering,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.230
at least for visual
effects, is still CPU-based,

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.134
and we're able to offer
this for burst rendering.

00:00:37.134 --> 00:00:39.300
But GPU is something that's
definitely on our minds.

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:45.560
JAMES COULTER: With
regard to deadline,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.680
absolutely we're supporting GPU.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.640
It's supported on a
sort of per renderer

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.590
or per application basis
within our plug-in system.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.636
But if the application
supports GPUs,

00:00:56.636 --> 00:00:58.260
we can implement it
within our plug-in.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.718
We already have
some-- for example,

00:00:59.718 --> 00:01:02.920
V-Ray and so forth-- and so
you can allocate how many GPUs

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.990
and where they go and so forth.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.000
So yes, it is there.

00:01:10.774 --> 00:01:12.940
HUNTER WILLIAMS: We would
love to see GPUs, as well.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:16.900
TODD PRIVES: I would
say we're actively

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.684
keeping tabs on where
the VFX market is

00:01:18.684 --> 00:01:19.600
in terms of rendering.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.280
And right now, we're looking
at V-Ray, Mental Ray, Arnold,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.940
Renderman primarily
being CPU-based

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.666
but, certainly, as that
progresses in the future,

00:01:26.666 --> 00:01:29.040
as [? Bel ?] said, we're
looking at everything at Google.

00:01:31.397 --> 00:01:32.480
AUDIENCE: That seems cool.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.420
Thanks for the presentation.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.600
For on-demand
licensing, for example

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.140
if we used a solution
like Syncbox,

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:48.320
the on-demand licensing for
plug-ins and V-Ray and Maya,

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.270
I know Zinc has it, but does
it, like, for example, like,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.745
for a solution
like Syncbox exist?

00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:56.300
JAMES COULTER: Sure.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:00.640
So obviously, the Thinkbox
model is sort of roll your own.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.900
And we try to make it as easy
as possible to roll your own.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.280
Clearly, the vendors
have to be on board

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.760
with on-demand licensing.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.509
We're providing our
own system that anyone

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:12.092
can jump onto if
they want to, so that

00:02:12.092 --> 00:02:13.180
makes it easier for them.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.690
Some will and some won't.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.940
But I think it's
undeniable, based

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.059
on what we've all seen today,
that if you're not playing

00:02:20.059 --> 00:02:21.600
nice with the cloud,
you're not going

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.650
to be all that successful.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:27.250
So a lot of vendors
already have solutions

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.800
that make on-demand
licensing work on the cloud.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.880
And the ones that don't,
I'd be very surprised

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.850
if they don't get
on board very soon.

00:02:46.694 --> 00:02:48.690
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.714
Question for me on-- and this
is just a general statement

00:02:51.714 --> 00:02:53.630
if any of you guys have
experiences with this,

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.859
because we're trying to
go through it as well.

00:02:55.859 --> 00:02:58.400
Have you had to deal with any
of the studios and the security

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.810
requirements for putting
these images up and down

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.030
in any kind of-- what have
they had you go through

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.562
for qualifications
and anything else?

00:03:06.562 --> 00:03:08.270
Just because this is
such a new platform,

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.260
you know, and there's
really not any kind

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.360
of standards or
any kind of letters

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.610
written on how to do this yet.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.068
So I didn't know
if any of you guys

00:03:15.068 --> 00:03:17.380
had any real-world experience
with the studios putting

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.910
their images into the
cloud and not being secure

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:21.982
in that facility.

00:03:21.982 --> 00:03:22.940
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Yeah.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.950
I mean, one, there's going to be
a completely different section

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.900
on security data
just off the launch.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.690
But the point is, [INAUDIBLE]
every studio considers

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.570
these assets as prized jewels.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:36.935
Their business depends on it.

00:03:36.935 --> 00:03:39.400
But it's also important to
know that for cloud, this

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.220
is our business.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.730
I'm needing to make sure
that our customers find

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.990
that their content
is safe on the cloud.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.410
And we make all efforts that
we can to keep it that way.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.790
At the same time, the
industry, I'm sure most of you

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.180
will agree, is quite fragmented.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.410
So what are those security
best practices that should be?

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.260
Each studio has its own view.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.190
And the rating for
MPAA guidelines

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:10.200
to be published so that we can
demonstrate that we not only

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.240
comply but exceed the
requirements of security

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.686
best practices.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:18.080
What I can probably say
is that for all the work

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.350
that on-prem security
engineers do,

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.170
the cloud as [? industry ?]
because we depend

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:29.030
so much on that business, what's
several orders more in terms

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.980
of investments in security.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.280
So that's what I can see.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.810
But I'd like to hear from
what studios like Rodeo FX

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.520
have actually
faced when they use

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.571
cloud for the real
stunning visual effects.

00:04:44.571 --> 00:04:46.570
JORDAN SOLES: I mean,
whenever we use the cloud,

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.210
we have to get in touch
with the studio right away

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.550
and get permission.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.140
And, surprise, surprise,
almost all of them say no.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.400
So yeah.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.320
So right now our cloud
usage is typically

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.810
focused on commercial
products or independent films

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.400
where the restrictions
become significantly less.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:12.070
HUNTER WILLIAMS: So we're taking
sort of a two-pronged approach.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:15.130
One is reactive and
one is proactive.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.340
On the reactive side, we do
work with a number of studios

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.610
out there that have blessed us
to do certain work for them.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:27.330
There are some ways that we
can not store data in the cloud

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.260
but maybe store it on site and
simply process frames and then

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.741
send them right back.

00:05:31.741 --> 00:05:34.240
On the proactive side, we're
working with the Cloud Security

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.020
Alliance and the
MPAA to understand

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.000
what the guidelines should be
and what the best practices

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.954
should be for security.

00:05:42.954 --> 00:05:46.370
AUDIENCE: You recognize, though,
that the MPAA is the lowest

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:47.725
bar.

00:05:47.725 --> 00:05:48.600
HUNTER WILLIAMS: Yes.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.440
Yes, I do.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.065
SRIKANTH BELWADI:
Yeah, actually, we're

00:05:53.065 --> 00:05:55.930
hoping that the bar would
increase because traditionally

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:57.960
MPAA probably was
tasked with this time

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.070
to explore physical security
of existing studios.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.851
AUDIENCE: Both the MPAA
and [INAUDIBLE] studios

00:06:03.851 --> 00:06:06.310
don't have a similar position.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:13.550
It's the [INAUDIBLE] That has to
figure into your negotiations.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:15.450
HUNTER WILLIAMS: No, I'm good.

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.344
JAMES COULTER: So
I think there's

00:06:17.344 --> 00:06:18.760
two different
pieces, though, when

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.240
you're talking about security.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.415
One is, is the
cloud itself secure?

00:06:23.415 --> 00:06:25.290
I think it's unquestionable
that it is secure

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.850
because banks are running on
it, military is running on it,

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:30.740
movies are being
distributed over the cloud.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:36.131
The cloud is secure
as a service.

00:06:36.131 --> 00:06:38.380
But then there's the question
of how you interface it?

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.040
How do you get from
your local premise

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.840
up to the cloud and
the entry points,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.080
endpoint attacks, and
so forth like that?

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.570
That really falls back to
just traditional IT security

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.030
that's fairly well understood.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:52.113
Now there's, of course, a
few new pieces in there.

00:06:52.113 --> 00:06:55.770
But security, of course, is
one of our highest concerns.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.680
But I don't think it's
as big of an enigma

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.877
as people make it out
to be, personally.

00:07:00.877 --> 00:07:02.708
AUDIENCE: I don't
disagree with you.

00:07:02.708 --> 00:07:06.194
But I don't think that
the studios with the MPAA,

00:07:06.194 --> 00:07:08.684
for that matter, were
based on rationality.

00:07:08.684 --> 00:07:11.174
They're an entirely
reactive model.

00:07:11.174 --> 00:07:12.170
OK?

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:14.150
If something gets
out into the wild,

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.321
they start bleeding evil as
quickly as they can find it.

00:07:17.321 --> 00:07:20.064
The cloud is [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:20.064 --> 00:07:21.730
JAMES COULTER: And
what you've just said

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.360
describes the internet
in general, right?

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.870
I mean, we went through
all this with the internet.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:26.660
We went through all
of it with VHS tapes.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:30.382
We went through all of it with
any sort of new distribution

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:31.840
mechanism that's
ever been invented

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.540
goes through this same
educational learning pain

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:35.220
process.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.116
And we're just now coming
to it with the studios.

00:07:38.116 --> 00:07:39.574
And we'll get
through it, you know.

00:07:39.574 --> 00:07:41.616
We're all smart people.

00:07:41.616 --> 00:07:42.612
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Yes.

00:07:42.612 --> 00:07:43.320
JACK: [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.720
SRIKANTH BELWADI:
Can I give you one?

00:07:53.074 --> 00:07:54.538
Oh, this one's--

00:07:54.538 --> 00:07:56.978
JACK: Hello?

00:07:56.978 --> 00:07:57.649
Enlighten me.

00:07:57.649 --> 00:07:58.940
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Oh, it's you.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:00.420
JACK: OK now.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:01.220
Oh.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:02.130
OK, my name is Jack.

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.467
I work with Uncharted Territory.

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:05.800
And the gentleman over there,
I worked actually with him

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.060
before.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:10.240
Security is dictated
to us by the studios.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.140
And specifically, I can tell you
when I worked at that company

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:16.240
before, Marvel dictated
to us what we can do,

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:17.070
what we cannot do.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:20.530
And I can assure you, they
did not like the cloud.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:21.980
In fact, they even
dictated to us

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.850
not to have internet access
to the artist workstations.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:26.510
So security is very important.

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.670
I work right now on a
[INAUDIBLE] feature movie.

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:30.670
I know for sure cloud is a no.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:32.520
I would like to
hear from you guys.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.340
One thing is, have
you worked with them

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.630
on getting the green light
that we can use the cloud?

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:40.220
Secondly, who's taking the
liability in case such as,

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.710
for example, "Expendables
3" was leaked last year

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:46.480
in August, full DVD quality,
three weeks before the release.

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:49.650
Is Google taking the liability
for that if something like that

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:50.150
happens?

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:52.530
Or is it going to be
us, the studios who's

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:53.800
working on the content?

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.285
Or is it the studios themselves?

00:08:59.352 --> 00:09:01.660
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Actually,
I'm seeing the very smart--

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:02.450
JACK: We got our--

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:02.770
SRIKANTH BELWADI: --security.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.770
Judy or Matt
O'Connor or somebody.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.070
Matt O, thank you very much.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:06.570
Thank you.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.310
You could have come
in just in time.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:14.450
Before I hand over
the mic to Matt,

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:17.370
we are trying to rationalize
with all the studios, including

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:20.810
Marvel, to look at
cloud wholistically

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:23.510
rather than having it yes
or no binary decisions.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:25.410
Right?

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.720
Regarding "Expendables,"
liability, frankly,

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.410
I do not know if any of that
has actually happened on it.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:32.880
OK, cool.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.007
Thank you, Matt. [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:36.007 --> 00:09:37.090
MATTHEW O'CONNOR: Working?

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:37.770
Oh, there we go.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.186
OK, there's a
little bit of delay.

00:09:39.186 --> 00:09:42.770
In case I say a curse word,
they can beat me, I guess.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.780
My name is Matthew
O'Connor, and I'm

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:46.780
responsible for security
and compliance products

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.170
for Google's Cloud Platform.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:50.354
And I do actually have
a prepared presentation

00:09:50.354 --> 00:09:52.020
to give you guys after
lunch and put you

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.890
to sleep after you've eaten.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.563
But very quickly.

00:09:57.563 --> 00:09:59.104
And we can talk
about this some more.

00:09:59.104 --> 00:10:01.395
And I'm around all afternoon,
so I can get into details

00:10:01.395 --> 00:10:04.080
with people who want.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.930
Yes, Marvel has a
lot of constraints

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:07.580
on what they want to do.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.040
We've talked to Marvel.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:10.880
We've talked to other studios.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:17.620
It's not really clear across
the motion picture industry

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.410
what a baseline standard is.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.130
And I'll show you
a little bit later

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.470
when I give my presentation
that we address

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:25.810
a lot of security standards.

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:27.420
And on some
industry-specific stuff

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.211
that has nothing to do
with motion pictures

00:10:29.211 --> 00:10:30.860
but might be applicable.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:37.310
We have-- actually, I was at
a content protection summit

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:38.760
before the holidays.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.040
And I actually made an
offer there in the room--

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.640
and this offer still
stands-- that Google

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:46.460
is willing to help
facilitate the conversation

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:50.380
with the motion picture
industry to figure out

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:52.370
what that baseline standard is.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:54.370
And we'd actually be
willing to do it in a way

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:56.661
where we could also bring
the competitors to the table.

00:10:56.661 --> 00:11:00.032
Because we don't believe
that we're the only solution

00:11:00.032 --> 00:11:01.240
that people are going to use.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:06.100
But we do believe that
cloud-based computing has

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:09.540
the ability to help
your industry do

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:12.280
some revolutionary things, do
new things, do things cheaper,

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.480
do things faster than
you've been able to do it.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:20.170
And I think there's some
content that you guys are

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:22.880
going to be able to create
that's going to blow our minds.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:24.380
But you're going
to be able to do it

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:28.129
because of the on-demand
nature of cloud resources.

00:11:28.129 --> 00:11:29.170
So we've made that offer.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.030
That offer still stands.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:35.490
And then specifics in terms of
liability and indemnification.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.871
We have commercial arrangements
with our customers.

00:11:38.871 --> 00:11:43.830
And so obviously, if there is a
major motion picture being done

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:46.040
and there's some
concerns about that,

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:50.190
I think Google, in terms of
being a commercial service

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.917
provider, we have to address
those kinds of things

00:11:52.917 --> 00:11:55.000
in our commercial arrangements
with our customers.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.640
And so we have some
provisions in our agreements

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.950
with customers that take care
of those kinds of things.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:02.750
Because we have banks.

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:05.590
We have companies that
have intellectual property

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:10.970
around industrial
mechanical design.

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:15.280
We have customers who are
delivering consumer services,

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.400
and the privacy of that
consumer information

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:18.900
is really, really
important to them.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:21.700
And so we protect
a broad spectrum

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:26.490
of customers, both through
our security measures,

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:28.000
security policies,
but also through

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.130
our commercial arrangements
with the customers.

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:32.350
And so we get lawyers.

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:34.891
We sit down and we hash that
out and get it worked out.

00:12:34.891 --> 00:12:36.390
And we can do it
in a way that it'll

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:39.887
be amenable to all
the parties involved.

00:12:39.887 --> 00:12:41.720
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Thank
you very much, Matt.

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:42.105
MATTHEW O'CONNOR: Sure.

00:12:42.105 --> 00:12:44.150
SRIKANTH BELWADI: And I do look
forward to your presentation

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:44.775
this afternoon.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:52.780
AUDIENCE: So I have a few
concerns and some questions.

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:55.950
The first concerns are
generally for the studios

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:58.640
in terms of gauging the
amount of waste, in terms

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:00.240
of compute time.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.910
It's not too often that artists
will send jobs to the farm.

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.250
And not because of
anything that they did,

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.600
but because maybe the
software that they're using

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:08.750
may fail on the farm.

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:12.720
Have you guys done some analysis
of some of those compute cycles

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.860
that may not have needed that
burn through hours and dollars,

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.080
of course, in terms
of associating

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:22.540
how they, with
licensed software--

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:25.230
part of the reason why we roll
our own cloud a lot of times

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:28.000
is because, again, it keeps
that liability a lot lower.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:29.960
And then the second
question is really

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:34.600
about alternatives to Evaer
and whatnot, things like Aspera

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.460
and Google's take
on things like that.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:39.010
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Sure.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:44.067
Maybe Hunter or Jordan, want
to take this cost and savings?

00:13:44.067 --> 00:13:44.983
HUNTER WILLIAMS: Sure.

00:13:44.983 --> 00:13:45.949
TODD PRIVES: Oh.

00:13:45.949 --> 00:13:47.398
[INAUDIBLE]

00:13:47.398 --> 00:13:48.364
OK. [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:48.364 --> 00:13:49.940
SRIKANTH BELWADI:
I'd be happy to.

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:51.440
TODD PRIVES: I mean,
I think there's

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.110
some inherent
responsibility to the user

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:54.920
if you're using software
and you submit a job

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:56.550
and there's a bug in
the host software.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.008
So that is something
that's come up

00:13:58.008 --> 00:14:00.740
a few times within our system.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:02.680
We're certainly happy
to work with customers

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:05.970
if it's a bug on the
infrastructure side.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.850
It's a little bit less clear,
and it hasn't come up that much

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:09.450
in terms of--

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.460
AUDIENCE: That's definitely
the studio's liability cost.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:11.700
TODD PRIVES: Right.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:12.658
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:14:12.658 --> 00:14:14.540
And that's inherently
part of the risk

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.852
you're associated with
with cloud at this point.

00:14:16.852 --> 00:14:18.310
But to be honest
with you, at least

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:20.370
in the Zinc
infrastructure standpoint,

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:22.335
we haven't seen that
being a major block.

00:14:22.335 --> 00:14:24.391
And it's probably the
same with Conductor

00:14:24.391 --> 00:14:26.890
in terms of the churn of hours
and the relatively few errors

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:29.230
that you actually see on shots.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:31.720
Granted, if an artist doesn't
prepare the shot properly

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:34.574
and the sampling is super
high, I mean, there's

00:14:34.574 --> 00:14:35.740
some responsibility on them.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:37.156
But in terms of
the software side,

00:14:37.156 --> 00:14:40.710
I haven't seen that high
of a failure rate where

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:42.130
it cuts into the actual spend.

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:44.004
But I mean, I would
imagine it's the same.

00:14:44.004 --> 00:14:44.920
HUNTER WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:47.972
So there's a couple of things
that we do to manage that.

00:14:47.972 --> 00:14:50.430
The first is that we do health
checks on all the instances.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:52.780
So we stream the log
files off of them that we

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.510
can detect anomalies in.

00:14:55.510 --> 00:14:57.610
The other is that we are
constantly going around

00:14:57.610 --> 00:14:58.820
and checking these instances.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:03.040
So if their memory spikes or
we see a panic and the instance

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:06.092
dies, you would still
be getting charged

00:15:06.092 --> 00:15:08.175
for a persistent disk and
some of the other things

00:15:08.175 --> 00:15:09.860
that are going on there.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:11.586
But we're going around
every 20 seconds

00:15:11.586 --> 00:15:13.210
and checking to see
where everything is

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:15.010
and if it's all working.

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:18.540
And we also have, as I talked
about in my presentation,

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:20.690
we have some bread
crumbs in our code

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:23.890
all around so that when any
sort of an action is taken,

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:25.390
it writes something
out to BigQuery,

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:26.764
an item out there,
so that we can

00:15:26.764 --> 00:15:30.510
track any job that comes on and
to make sure that it comes off.

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:33.160
And while it's
running, we can track

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:35.900
to see if anything
has happened to it.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:37.572
So when it comes
to runaway costs,

00:15:37.572 --> 00:15:39.280
we try to mitigate
that as much as we can

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:43.130
by just constantly looking back
and seeing if things are still

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:46.434
running correctly and
constantly just health

00:15:46.434 --> 00:15:48.475
checking all the instances
across the whole farm.

00:15:50.667 --> 00:15:52.250
TODD PRIVES: And
just one other thing.

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:53.500
I don't envision
it that differently

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.040
if you have an artist
that submits a job locally

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:57.186
and you are paying something
for your HVAC and for your power

00:15:57.186 --> 00:15:57.860
to that machine.

00:15:57.860 --> 00:15:59.770
And if it's a wasted
render, that's

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:01.540
still a sunk cost to you.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.002
So I don't envision it
that much differently.

00:16:04.002 --> 00:16:04.960
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Yeah.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:08.675
Regarding your second question
about Aspera and Evaer.

00:16:08.675 --> 00:16:11.900
We are under no illusion that
all of visual effects rendering

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:14.750
are likely to happen
on cloud from day one.

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:16.650
People have existing
data centers

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:20.100
and will probably use
cloud for bursting.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:23.420
So with that being said,
I think our partnership

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:25.960
that we are just recently
announcing that Evaer

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.977
is actually very important.

00:16:27.977 --> 00:16:30.310
People probably already have
their existing compliances,

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:31.684
and we have just
recently come up

00:16:31.684 --> 00:16:37.110
with the virtual filer
that runs on GCP.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:40.660
Similarly, Aspera
[INAUDIBLE] with the offering

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:45.230
does support [INAUDIBLE]
files from your desk jobs

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:49.266
or from your local data centers
to Google's Cloud Platform.

00:16:49.266 --> 00:16:52.202
And those integration
farms are what we enable.

00:16:59.639 --> 00:17:00.180
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:01.400
This is for Jordan.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:04.310
Could you elaborate more
on your SaltStack setup

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:07.163
and talk about maybe
the time to boot

00:17:07.163 --> 00:17:09.079
to when the system's
actually ready to render.

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:13.630
JORDAN SOLES: Not really.

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:14.547
I don't have the--

00:17:14.547 --> 00:17:15.130
[MIC FEEDBACK]

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:15.669
Oops, sorry.

00:17:15.669 --> 00:17:17.770
I don't have those stats.

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:23.700
I forget what our-- I mean,
we had done instant time setup

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:28.730
prior to that with VMs, and
the time it would take to boot

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:34.525
was just not great in
comparison to what Google claims

00:17:34.525 --> 00:17:36.430
the instances can start with.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.920
As soon as we
started using Salt,

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:42.570
everything started getting
closer to the Google times

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:44.592
that we had seen before.

00:17:44.592 --> 00:17:48.100
But I'd be happy to put you in
touch with somebody at Rodeo

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:50.415
if you have any
interest in that.

00:17:50.415 --> 00:17:51.040
AUDIENCE: Cool.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:51.540
Thank you.

00:17:56.874 --> 00:17:59.290
AUDIENCE: I have a question,
I guess it's about logistics.

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:06.084
Just like a basic
logistical question.

00:18:06.084 --> 00:18:07.500
Do we have any
real-world examples

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.300
from any of you guys
or anyone else here

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:13.422
of moving data back
and forth to the cloud?

00:18:13.422 --> 00:18:14.880
For us, that's our
biggest concern,

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.712
being able to ingest the
data quickly, especially

00:18:17.712 --> 00:18:19.170
like a company like
I work for now.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:21.200
We're working on a lot
of smaller projects.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:25.050
Instead of working on one
film for a year or two years,

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:28.190
we're working on a couple
hundred smaller projects.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:30.275
So that moving data
back and forth,

00:18:30.275 --> 00:18:32.410
it's sometimes
terabytes per day.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:34.940
We don't have the
pipe to do that.

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:38.510
Security and speed
don't get along.

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:42.550
So I'm curious just if anyone
has any real-world examples

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:43.898
dealing with that.

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:49.964
JAMES COULTER:
Generally speaking,

00:18:49.964 --> 00:18:52.130
it always comes back to how
much bandwidth you have.

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:53.810
If you have large bandwidth,
you have a little bit more

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:54.660
flexibility.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:57.470
You always want to be
intelligent with choosing which

00:18:57.470 --> 00:19:00.140
files to push up and push down.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.800
Compositing is often challenging
unless the data is already

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:06.082
up there, of course,
simply because you

00:19:06.082 --> 00:19:07.790
have to move your
plates up and so forth.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:10.520
In a lot of cases, if you're
looking at-- simulations

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.680
are a great example, where you
have a fairly small set of data

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.159
that you're uploading.

00:19:15.159 --> 00:19:16.950
You may be generating
cash or your download

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:19.040
might be kind of heavy,
but generally speaking,

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:22.350
a lot of facilities have an
easier time getting data down

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:23.710
than they do pushing data up.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:26.140
So you can push up data
sets that are small,

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:30.080
do your compute, pull the data
set down, or alternatively,

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:32.070
leave it up there
for the next stage.

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.400
So it is doable.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:37.370
It's like so many other
things in computing,

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:39.390
you just have to be
aware of the constraints

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:43.480
and send the right work, use
the right tool for the job,

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:45.974
essentially.

00:19:45.974 --> 00:19:47.342
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:47.342 --> 00:19:50.746
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Should we
vote for online use case as well

00:19:50.746 --> 00:19:52.320
as offline ingestion.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:55.500
Ingestion is by far the biggest
problem for cloud computing

00:19:55.500 --> 00:20:00.120
because processing will happen
only if the content is there.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:02.640
So for the online,
the real strength

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.570
is the Google Massive Network
and [INAUDIBLE] of presence.

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:07.970
And recently announced
carrier interconnect.

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:11.980
So irrespective of which telecom
operator that you work with,

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:14.200
we will be able
to peer and we'll

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:16.104
be able to offer
the interconnect

00:20:16.104 --> 00:20:18.470
with your provider.

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:22.140
We just started a [INAUDIBLE]
offline ingestion right here

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:24.750
in LA with a partner
called Iron Mountain.

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:27.650
So if you have disks of content
and you need to upload it,

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:31.031
you don't need to do
it from your device.

00:20:31.031 --> 00:20:33.470
You can actually ship it
or carry it [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:37.690
TODD PRIVES: I do want to add.

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:40.040
I think that the perception
is worse than the reality,

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:41.470
oftentimes, with CG rendering.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:42.720
I mean, at the
beginning of a project

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:45.011
is when your textures and
your large assets are coming.

00:20:45.011 --> 00:20:48.137
So if you manage your pipeline
well, and Zinc does it

00:20:48.137 --> 00:20:49.720
and I'm sure Conductor
does it, you're

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:52.060
able to parse a scene for
only the assets you need.

00:20:52.060 --> 00:20:54.060
So as long as you're able
to move that stuff up,

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:56.850
generally in the day or two
or three that you're animating

00:20:56.850 --> 00:20:58.640
or you're modeling
or doing geometry,

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:00.340
those large assets
are not changing.

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:02.099
And I know it's a
foreign concept.

00:21:02.099 --> 00:21:04.140
You guys have to move
these massive data sets up.

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:06.460
But we found when we had
hundreds of customers

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:09.090
prior to joining Amazon--
joining Google app when

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.830
we were on Amazon, we have
many customers now in the beta.

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:15.110
And data I/O, I think,
is perceived initially

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:16.400
as a major blocker.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:18.935
But once you're actually up
and in the pipeline using it,

00:21:18.935 --> 00:21:19.780
it's not as bad.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:21.383
And I would imagine it's
the same for-- you guys put

00:21:21.383 --> 00:21:22.120
a whole feature film through.

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:23.360
I would imagine it's the same.

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:24.276
HUNTER WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:21:24.276 --> 00:21:26.290
We call them
incremental uploads.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:27.820
Anything that changes
gets uploaded.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:29.200
When we do the
dependency checks,

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:32.880
anything that's been uploaded
already we just skip.

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.760
And then to confirm
that it's going to work,

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.960
we pre-render the first, the
middle, and the last frame,

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:41.076
just to make sure that
all the items are there.

00:21:41.076 --> 00:21:42.700
AUDIENCE: I think
that the offline idea

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:44.450
is really good for
smaller companies

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.075
and for studios around
Los Angeles that

00:21:46.075 --> 00:21:47.610
are getting their feet wet.

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:49.860
Where we're still having to
ingest terabytes per day,

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:53.080
the ability to send a
runner over with hard drives

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:56.244
is the reality of how we're
going to have to work.

00:21:56.244 --> 00:21:57.160
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Yep.

00:22:11.714 --> 00:22:12.380
AUDIENCE: Hello?

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:13.540
OK.

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:15.590
I work with a company
that helps companies build

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:18.430
on-prem solutions, so I'm privy
to a lot of the constraints

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:19.914
that these guys deal with.

00:22:19.914 --> 00:22:21.580
And there are a lot
of questions I have.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:22.650
But the one question
I've got is,

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:24.941
we're always trying to figure
out that inflection point

00:22:24.941 --> 00:22:26.510
of maximizing CPU utilization.

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:28.010
And we run into a
lot of bottlenecks

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:29.750
whether it's a network
or the storage.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:31.560
So one thing I was
wondering is if there's

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:34.090
any telemetry in the VMs
that you guys are running

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.680
in the cloud that will tell you
if the CPU is running at 100%

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:38.860
and if there's any way to
look at what the storage

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:41.624
protocols that are being used
are to make sure there's not

00:22:41.624 --> 00:22:42.790
a bottleneck somewhere else.

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:44.581
It's one thing to have
a VM that's running,

00:22:44.581 --> 00:22:45.494
that's processing.

00:22:45.494 --> 00:22:47.410
But to know if it's being
managed efficiently,

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:49.890
if the storage on the
back end is optimized

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:51.730
to handle that type of flow.

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:54.800
Is there anything that you
guys could talk about that?

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:57.560
JAMES COULTER: So I'll speak
briefly about deadline.

00:22:57.560 --> 00:22:59.150
Generally speaking,
a VM is going

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.620
to behave the same way that
a physical machine does.

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:04.310
So whatever utilization you
see on a physical machine,

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:05.740
it's going to mirror on a VM.

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:07.900
Additionally, in the
case of deadline,

00:23:07.900 --> 00:23:12.290
we have metrics that we
gather from the machine

00:23:12.290 --> 00:23:14.560
so that you can graph
out the CPU utilization

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:15.980
over the course of the job.

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:16.985
So it's there.

00:23:16.985 --> 00:23:17.610
You can see it.

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:21.350
And then make your
determinations based on that.

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:23.870
And then, of course,
deadline load

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:27.330
balances the number of VMs
based on the load in the queue

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:29.750
so that you get the same
sort of efficiency you

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:32.650
do with a lot of the solutions
that you're seeing here,

00:23:32.650 --> 00:23:35.570
where you're only amping
up the number of cores

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:38.702
that you really
need to do the work.

00:23:38.702 --> 00:23:40.410
SRIKANTH BELWADI: And
I'd like to mention

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:43.960
the recent stack driver
product that's integrated

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:45.300
with the Google Cloud Platform.

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:49.110
But you can monitor not
just the Google instances

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:53.904
but across any of your
public cloud offering

00:23:53.904 --> 00:23:58.780
if you are basically
using multiple clouds.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:01.435
AUDIENCE: What is the back end
storage protocol that directory

00:24:01.435 --> 00:24:04.007
is using, for example?

00:24:04.007 --> 00:24:05.590
Where's the data
stored, and how is it

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:08.130
communicating with the VMs?

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:11.370
HUNTER WILLIAMS: So it
lives in a mostly a cluster

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:13.550
system with PD behind it.

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:18.100
We use Google's I/O scales
with how big the PD is.

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:22.100
So we get to the right size
to get the max I/O out of it.

00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:26.720
And then we mount volume after
volume and cluster instance

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:27.739
after cluster instance.

00:24:27.739 --> 00:24:29.780
AUDIENCE: So the back end
scales as the front end

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:31.025
is scaling too?

00:24:31.025 --> 00:24:31.900
HUNTER WILLIAMS: Yes.

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.620
JACOB: If I can make a
suggestion regarding I/O. Maybe

00:24:41.620 --> 00:24:44.100
the Google Cloud
Platform guys could

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:46.130
interface with the
Google Fiber guys

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:48.850
and get an LA deployment
pushed out here.

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:50.136
That would be pretty nice.

00:24:50.136 --> 00:24:50.636
Thanks.

00:24:50.636 --> 00:24:52.094
SRIKANTH BELWADI:
Thank you, Jacob.

00:25:00.688 --> 00:25:01.188
Yep.

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:07.870
AUDIENCE: So I agree
with Google Fiber.

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:09.220
We need that in San Diego too.

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:10.630
It's terrible there.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:14.340
So about all the software and
plug-ins, et cetera, et cetera.

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:18.500
I don't understand exactly
how the deployment works.

00:25:18.500 --> 00:25:22.910
For example, in my setup
is Max, Fusion, V-Ray,

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:25.620
and a bunch of plug-ins.

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:29.690
So when you create the virtual
machine the first time,

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:33.260
you install the
software, and then stays

00:25:33.260 --> 00:25:37.140
there, that virtual machine
available for you forever.

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:37.870
How does it work?

00:25:37.870 --> 00:25:40.140
How does it apply updates,
et cetera, et cetera?

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:43.730
How does it work in
the case of Zinc?

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:45.982
I see that it works
with Maya and NUKE,

00:25:45.982 --> 00:25:48.440
there's a possibility that it
will work with other software

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:50.370
too, or that's the limitation?

00:25:52.537 --> 00:25:54.120
TODD PRIVES: I mean,
on the Zinc side,

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:55.630
it's billed as a
turnkey solution,

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:58.690
so we're looking at other host
applications in the future.

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.480
But essentially now,
when you submit a job

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:02.730
from an application,
we'll parse the scene,

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:04.960
make sure we have the proper
version of the software,

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:06.840
of the render, of the
plug-ins installed,

00:26:06.840 --> 00:26:10.510
and have an image ready for you,
a preconfigured image for you,

00:26:10.510 --> 00:26:11.270
based on that.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:14.590
I would imagine it's the
same with Conductor as well.

00:26:14.590 --> 00:26:17.700
So on software as
a service side,

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:20.150
that's how we have these
images ready for you.

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:23.010
Analyze your scene and
bake out one for you.

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:26.050
AUDIENCE: So would you
work at Max and Fusion?

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:28.300
TODD PRIVES: Currently we
don't support Max or Fusion.

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:31.120
But again, we're looking at all
content creation applications

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:32.612
in the future.

00:26:32.612 --> 00:26:33.320
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:38.610
JAMES COULTER: And so
in the case of deadline,

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:41.985
you would essentially
create your own OS image.

00:26:41.985 --> 00:26:42.485
Right?

00:26:42.485 --> 00:26:43.540
So exactly what you said.

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:45.890
You pull up an instance
of the operating

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:49.271
system that's provided
by Google, install

00:26:49.271 --> 00:26:50.270
whatever you want on it.

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:52.810
Essentially you install
your same pipeline

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:55.840
that you would in your facility,
and then you save that out

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:56.714
as an image.

00:26:56.714 --> 00:26:58.630
And then, when it's
spawning virtual machines,

00:26:58.630 --> 00:27:01.900
it's just making copies of that
image for each virtual machine.

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:04.607
And so you can pretty much build
exactly what pipeline you want.

00:27:04.607 --> 00:27:06.190
Of course, you have
to be a little bit

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:08.410
intelligent about
your bandwidth.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:10.400
You don't want to do
anything too heavy

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:13.110
based on your bandwidth between
your local and your cloud.

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:15.060
But in a lot of cases,
a solution to that

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:17.606
is just push whatever
database or whatever

00:27:17.606 --> 00:27:18.980
it is you think
you're talking to

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:20.982
or whatever piece is
slow up into the cloud

00:27:20.982 --> 00:27:22.982
so that it can scale with
the rest of the stuff.

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:31.445
